l A. Nemeyer, Scholarly Reprint Publishing in the United States, New York, N.Y.: R. R. Bowker Co., →ISBN, page 8:"}, {"text": "Irregular bedtimes may disrupt healthy brain development in young children, according to a study of intelligence and sleeping habits. Going to bed at a different time each night affected girls more than boys, but both fared worse on mental tasks than children who had a set bedtime, researchers found.", "reference": "2013 July 19, Ian Sample, “Irregular bedtimes may affect children's brains”, in The Guardian Weekly, volume 189, number 6, page 34:"}, {"text": "While long-distance and commuter rail travel still fared well, train travel to seaside resorts was perhaps inevitably falling away.", "reference": "2023 March 8, Howard Johnston, “Was Marples the real railway wreccker?”, in RAIL, number 978, page 51:"}], "synonyms": ["do", "get along", "manage", "cope"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To proceed or progress (in a certain way).", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["We will continue to monitor how the hurricane fares against projected models."], "quotes": [{"text": "He was a man of Spartan habits, and at sixty was scrupulous about his diet at your table, excusing himself by saying that he must eat sparingly and fare hard, as became a soldier or one who was fitting himself for difficult enterprises, a life of exposure.", "reference": "1859, Henry David Thoreau, A Plea for Captain John Brown:"}], "synonyms": ["do", "act", "behave", "measure up", "stack up"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(impersonal) To happen or occur (in a certain way).", "tags": ["impersonal", "intransitive"], "examples": ["We shall see how it fares with him."], "quotes": [{"text": "So fares it when with truth falsehood contends.", "reference": "1671, John Milton, “The First Book”, in Paradise Regain’d. A Poem. In IV Books. To which is Added, Samson Agonistes, London: […] J[ohn] M[acock] for John Starkey […], →OCLC, page 3:"}, {"text": "Let me only say that it fared with him as with the storm-tossed ship, that miserably drives along the leeward land.", "reference": "1851, Herman Melville, Moby Dick, chapter 23:"}], "synonyms": ["go", "turn out", "work out", "develop", "unfold"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(archaic) To go; to travel.", "tags": ["archaic", "intransitive"], "examples": ["Behold! A knight fares forth."], "quotes": [{"text": "[…]And fared like a furious wyld Beare, / Whose whelpes are stolne away, she being otherwhere.", "reference": "1596, Edmund Spenser, “Book VI, Canto XI”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "I know that this was Life,—the track\n⁠Whereon with equal feet we fared;\n⁠And then, as now, the day prepared\nThe daily burden for the back.", "reference": "1850, [Alfred, Lord Tennyson], “Canto XXV”, in In Memoriam, London: Edward Moxon, […], →OCLC, page 42:"}, {"text": "Then he came down rejoicing and said, \"I have seen what seemeth to be a city as 'twere a pigeon.\" Hereat we rejoiced and, ere an hour of the day had passed, the buildings showed plain in the offing and we asked the Captain, \"What is the name of yonder city?\" and he answered \"By Allah I wot not, for I never saw it before and never sailed these seas in my life: but, since our troubles have ended in safety, remains for you only to land their with your merchandise and, if you find selling profitable, sell and make your market of what is there; and if not, we will rest here two days and provision ourselves and fare away.", "reference": "1885, Richard F. Burton, The Book of the Thousand Nights and a Night, Night 17:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(archaic) To eat; to dine.", "tags": ["archaic", "intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "There was a certain rich man which […] fared sumptuously every day.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Luke 16:19:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fare", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}